  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=5,$ we plug $a=5$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(5)+{f}^{\u2032}(5)(x-5)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(5)(x-5)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(5),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(5),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(5):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{x} & \u27f9 &  & f(5) & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(5) & =-\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =2{x}^{-3} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(5) & =\\frac{2}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(5),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(5),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(5)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =(\\frac{6}{5})+(-\\frac{1}{25})\\cdot (x-5)+(\\frac{2}{125})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-5)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}-\\frac{1}{25}(x-5)+\\frac{1}{125}(x-5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
